The four points are the corners of the square with center $(i, j)$ and side length 1.
The coordinates of the corners are $(i \pm 0.5, j \pm 0.5)$.
The distance of a point $(x, y)$ from the origin is $\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$.
The condition is that for all four corners, $\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \le R$.
Since the square is convex, the point farthest from the origin among the four corners is one of the corners.
Let's check the distance squared for a corner $(x, y)$.
$x^2 + y^2 \le R^2$.
The corners are $(i+0.5, j+0.5)$, $(i+0.5, j-0.5)$, $(i-0.5, j+0.5)$, $(i-0.5, j-0.5)$.
The squared distance for $(i+0.5, j+0.5)$ is $(i+0.5)^2 + (j+0.5)^2$.
